course: dictionary
lesson: 8381
title: Many meanings of rub
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Rub» can mean moving your hand over something, spreading something on, removing pencil marks, or the act of rubbing itself.
explain: «A boy in Alaska tells his friend not to rub snow on her frozen cheeks.» Snow is cold and her cheeks are frozen, so rub means moving the snow backwards and forwards over her skin, pressing on it. Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Look at the words next to rub. «Rub in sun cream» means spread it on. «Rub out» means remove pencil marks with a rubber, which is the UK meaning. The words after rub are the clue.
fact: Rub can be a verb (you rub) or a noun (you give something a rub).
fact: To rub is to move your hand or something else backwards and forwards over a surface, pressing on it.
fact: In science, rubbing two things together makes friction and heat, and rubbing a balloon on hair makes static electricity.
fact: «Rub it in» means keep reminding someone of a mistake, and «rub someone up the wrong way» means annoy them.
fact: In health, do not rub your eyes with dirty hands.
exercise: «Friction acts between surfaces that rub and works against the movement.» What does rub mean here? | To laugh at someone | To wash something with water | *To move two surfaces backwards and forwards over each other, pressing | To tidy up a room
exercise: Dad says, «Rub in some sun cream before we go out.» What does rub in mean? | To keep reminding someone of a mistake | To remove the cream with a towel | *To spread the cream into the skin by rubbing | To wash the cream off
exercise: In the UK, what does «rub out» mean? | To shout loudly | *To remove pencil marks with a rubber | To spread paint on a wall | To polish a shoe
test: Jayden's friend says, «Don't rub it in, I already feel silly.» What does rub it in mean? | To clean it off | To spread it thinly | *To keep reminding someone of a mistake | To hide it under a cloth
test: «Where hands rub a brass door knob every day, the thin dull layer is rubbed off, and the metal stays bright.» Why does the knob stay bright? | Because brass never gets dirty | *Because hands rub off the thin dull layer | Because nobody touches it | Because it is painted every week
exam: Which sentence uses rub as a noun? | They rubbed sun cream on their backs | Please rub your hands before you eat | *She gave the old dog a gentle rub behind the ears | We rub the table clean every day
